    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d570">

                <pb facs="0991" n="887" sameAs="frus:frus1943CairoTehran/pg887"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">FE Files</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Co-Chairman of the Anglo-American
                        Caribbean Commission</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Taussig</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-01-16">January 16, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">hongkong</hi></p>
                <p>The President told Stanley<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d570-f1"/> in detail of his discussions on Hongkong with Churchill at Cairo. Briefly—the British to make
                    a <hi rendition="#italic">beau gest</hi>[<hi rendition="#italic">e</hi>]:
                    announce the return of Hongkong to China; Ch[i]ang “or his successor” in three
                    days time to announce that Hongkong is a free port and open to the entire world
                    on equal terms; no one in Hongkong to be deprived of his property as of the
                    period prior to the Japanese invasion. …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d570-f1" n="1" xml:id="d570-f1-content">
                <p>The reference is to Oliver F. G. Stanley,
                    the British Secretary of State for the Colonies, who was in the process of
                    discussing colonial problems with officials of the Department of State; see
                    Notter, <ref target="#pg389">p. 389</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
